  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Caught kissing Denis by Nikita's father Andrey Nikolaevich, Katya improvises on the spot and introduces Denis as her first cousin—a touring classical pianist about to fly to Tokyo to perform Tchaikovsky.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "С кем целуется будущая невестка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это Денис, мой двоюродный брат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А это папа моего Никиты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Мне тоже очень приятно познакомиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Может, тогда вместе сейчас поужинаем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не получится, Денис тут проездом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него сегодня вечером самолёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Куда путь держите, молодой человек?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я сегодня лечу в Японию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него там большие гастроли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис у нас талантливый пианист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Как же мне надоело врать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "С кем целуется будущая невестка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who is my future daughter-in-law kissing?' the guest anchors the line on 'with', while 'daughter-in-law' completes the thought. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это Денис, мой двоюродный брат.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is Denis, my first cousin.' The grammar packs 'this' and 'brother' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А это папа моего Никиты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And this is my Nikita's dad.' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'Nikita' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Мне тоже очень приятно познакомиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am also very pleased to meet you.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'to me' and finishing on 'to get acquainted'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Может, тогда вместе сейчас поужинаем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe we could have dinner together now?' the guest anchors the line on 'maybe', while 'to have dinner' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не получится, Денис тут проездом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It will not work out, Denis is passing through.' The grammar packs 'not' and 'passing through' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него сегодня вечером самолёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He has a plane this evening.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'plane' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Куда путь держите, молодой человек?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are you headed, young man?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'where to' before landing on 'man'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я сегодня лечу в Японию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am flying to Japan today.' Denis anchors the line on 'I', while 'Japan' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него там большие гастроли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He has a big concert tour there.' Russian expresses 'having' with the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun and 'concert tour'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис у нас талантливый пианист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Denis is a talented pianist of ours.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'Denis' and 'at' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Как же мне надоело врать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How tired I am of lying.' In Russian, the idea of 'how' flows naturally into 'to lie' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
